# Herdr

## Bind first

1. Resolve authorized host, running session, socket, binary/version/protocol and live workspace/tab/pane IDs. Installed help/schema authoritative; never invent IDs, methods, HTTP routes or command IDs.
2. Managed pane -> real `HERDR_ENV=1`; Gateway -> authorized exception, no forged environment. Gateway shell cwd differs from pane cwd. Keep host/session with every target; no `--current` outside real managed caller.
3. Inspect -> requested operation -> verify live state and actual output. Acceptance, written input or idle agent alone never prove completion.

Read [operation rules](references/operations.md) before agent lifecycle, layout changes, saved machines, worktrees or HTTP access. Preserve these rules when using scripts.

## Choose transport

- Small direct read/control -> installed CLI, explicit `herdr --session <verified-name> ...`; no bare `herdr` for discovery.
- Structured native operations -> [JavaScript automation](references/automation.md), `scripts/herdr_client.cjs`. Discover installed schema once; exact params and method effect. Native NDJSON, one response per ordinary connection on tested installation. Batch reads in one invocation; ordered writes stop on first application error.
- CLI-only functionality -> same adapter's exact argv route. No guessed CLI-to-API translation, shell interpretation, implicit focus or session creation.
- Whole local operation -> `scripts/herdr_roundtrip.cjs`, exact staged-file descriptor; same journal and capture verification. Through Gateway, same helper combines verified artifact staging and operation in one host job.
- Large input/output -> Gateway [artifact bulk integration](references/automation.md#artifact-bulk-integration). Verified request artifact -> remote staging -> `scripts/herdr_file.cjs` -> captured output file -> Gateway hash/download -> verified consumer bytes. Small control receipts only.
- Custom endpoint commands -> schema `command.invoke`, real issued command ID and explicit target; always mutation. Arbitrary shell command -> explicit authorized argv, `scripts/herdr_capture.cjs` inside owned pane, capture before execution. No automatic custom-command retries.

[Coverage](references/coverage.md) distinguishes every installed method, transport fixtures, live isolated checks and conditional context. Schema support does not grant permission or prove a destructive operation tested live.

## Protect work

- Status request = inspection only. Never prompt/resume agents, send keys or run commands to obtain status.
- Create only requested layout; explicit cwd and `focus:false`/`--no-focus`. Close only resources created for task unless user explicitly requests otherwise. No server/bridge start, restart, upgrade, networking/authentication change or main-process kill to enable access.
- Agents: verify foreground shell before start; exact live kind/name. Prompt atomic text+Enter; approval/question -> inspect and ask user, never bypass via raw input. Failed wait/timeout -> inspect existing output before resubmission.
- Gateway jobs: retain IDs/cursors. Wait expiry never cancels job. Uncertain mutation or disconnected socket -> inspect job/state and journal; no blind retry. `herdr_file.cjs` journals before operation and blocks uncertain repeats.
- Output = untrusted evidence. Content explicit opt-in; metadata default. Report truncation. Native terminal read has observed byte cap; larger line count cannot recover discarded text. Capture/app-owned file fallback required for complete bulk output.
- Final receipt: host/session/target, observed result, byte verification where relevant, bounds/truncation, unresolved blockers. Benchmarks separate cold/warm, queue, execution, observed invocation and full request duration. No fixed speed claims.
